Denote by  $X$  and  $Y$  the midpoints of  $BD$  and  $CE$  and by  $P$  the midpoint of  $BC$ .  Observe that  $IX=IP=IY$ .  Next, consider the second intersection  $T$  of  $(ABC)$  and  $(ADE)$ . It's the center of the spiral similarity  $BD \to CE$ , but then it must be an (oriented) congruence, so  $TB = TC \implies T \equiv M$ . Hence  $MX = MY$ ; thus  $MI$  is the perpendicular bisector of  $XY$ .

Now  $XY$  is the Gauss line of complete quadrilateral  $DBEC$ . Since  $I$  is the orthocenter of triangle  $FBC$ , line  $MI$  must be the Steiner line of  $DBEC$  (since the Steiner and Gauss lines are always perpendicular), which by definition passes through  $H$ .

