I'd just like to point out that \[ \sum_{|S|  \text{ odd}} f(S) - \frac{1}{2} = (-2)^{n-1} \prod_{k=1}^n  \left (a_k - \frac{1}{2}\right ). \]
Note that if  $a_1=\frac{1}{2}$ , then \begin{align*} \sum_{|S|  \text{ odd}} f(S) - \frac{1}{2} &= \frac{1}{2}\sum_{T\in \{2, ... , n\}} \left ( \prod_{i\in T} a_i \cdot \prod_{j \not \in T} (1-a_j) \right )- \frac{1}{2}  &=  \frac{1}{2} \prod_{k=2}^n \left (a_k + (1-a_k) \right ) - \frac{1}{2}  &= \frac{1}{2} - \frac{1}{2}  &= 0. \end{align*}
So  $a_1 - \frac{1}{2}$  is a factor of  $\sum_{|S|  \text{ odd}} f(S) - \frac{1}{2}$ . Then by symmetry  $a_2 - \frac{1}{2}, \cdots , a_n - \frac{1}{2}$  are also factors of it and since it is an n-degree polynomial, we only now seek a constant term of multiplication which can be evaluated by adjusting the constant term to be  $-\frac{1}{2}$ .

