<details><summary>Click to expand</summary>Notice that for  $m=n$  we can simply take  $P(k)=t^k, \forall 0\leq k\leq m$ . Hence we suppose, that  $m>n$ . Now also notice, that if  $\frac{P(k)}{t^{k+1}}=a$  is an integer, then  $\frac{P(k)}{t^k}=a\cdot t$  is also an integer, which is a contradiction and thus we have  $\frac{P(k)}{t^k}$  is an integer  $\forall 0\leq k \leq m$ . Therefore set  $P(k) = a_kt^k, \gcd(a_k,t)=1$ .

Now we use Lagrange Interpolation as follows:  $$ P(x) = \sum_{k=0}^mP(k)\prod_{l\neq k}\frac{x-l}{k-l} \, . $$  Since this would imply  $\deg P = m > n$ , we must have the coefficient of  $x^m$  equal to  $0$ : 
\begin{align*}
0&=\sum_{k=0}^mP(k)\prod_{l\neq k}\frac{1}{k-l}
&=\sum_{k=0}^m a_kt^k\prod_{l\neq k}\frac{1}{k-l}
&=\sum_{k=0}^ma_kt^k\frac{(-1)^{m-k}}{k!(m-k)!}
&=\sum_{k=0}^ma_kt^k {m \choose k} (-1)^{m-k}
\end{align*}
Now  $t$  divides this sum, especially each summand for  $k \geq 1$ . However, then  $t$  must divide the summand for  $k=0$ , i.e.  $t$  must divide  $a_0$ . But we know  $\gcd(a_k,t)=1, \forall 0\leq k \leq m$ , contradiction! We conclude that  $m=n$  is the maximal  $m$ .  $\square$</details>

