The formula for in the post above that I gave is interesting, but not necessary for the solution, but it would make a good challenge if someone can solve the problem using it.

Instead, this problem is easily solved by Taylor Series: $f(x)=\frac{\sin x}{x}=\sum_{k=0}^\infty\frac{(-1)^kx^{2k}}{(2k+1)!}$  $|f^{(n)}(x)|=\Bigg|\sum_{k=\lceil n/2\rceil}^\infty\frac{(-1)^k(2k)!x^{2k-n}}{(2k-n)!(2k+1)!}\Bigg|\leq\frac{1}{n+1}\bigg|\sum_{k=\lceil n/2\rceil}^\infty\frac{(-1)^kx^{2k-n}}{(2k-n)!}\bigg|\leq\frac{1}{n+1}
$ The last inequality follows because  $\sum_{k=\lceil n/2\rceil}^\infty\frac{(-1)^kx^{2k-n}}{(2k-n)!}$  is the Taylor Series of  $\pm\sin x$  is  $n$  is odd or  $\pm\cos x$  if  $n$  is even. Both are bounded above by  $1$  in absolute value.

