<u>Lemma:</u> Let  $I$  and  $J$  be the circumcenters of  $(SHC)$  and  $(THC)$  respectively. Then, the angle conditions imply that  $I\in AB$  and  $J\in AD$ .

<u>Proof of Lemma:</u> We see that
\[\angle ISC=\pi/2-\frac{1}{2}\angle SIC=\pi/2-(\pi-\angle SHC)=\angle BSC,\]
so  $I\in AB$ . Similarly,  $J\in AD$ .  $\blacksquare$ To show  $(SHT)$  tangent to  $BD$ , we want to show that the perpendicular bisectors of  $SH$  and  $TH$  intersect on  $AH$ . But these are the angle bisectors of  $\angle SIH=\angle AIH$  and  $\angle TJH=\angle AJH$  respectively. Therefore, by the angle bisector theorem on  $AIH$  and  $AJH$ , all we want to show is that  $AI/IH=AJ/JH$ , or  $IA/JA=IH/JH$ .

We claim that  $(AHC)$  is the  $A$ -Appolonius circle, which then finishes as  $IA/JA=IH/JH$ . Let  $D=(AHC)\cap IJ$ . We see that  $\angle DAH=\angle CAH$  since  $H$  and  $C$  are reflections in  $IJ$ , so  $AD$  is the angle bisector of  $\angle IAJ$ . But we know that  $AH$  and  $AC$  are isogonal in  $\angle IAJ$ , so  $D$  is the foot of the angle bisector. Therefore,  $(AHC)$  is a circle symmetric in  $AIJ$  that passes through the foot of the angle bisector on  $IJ$ , so it is the Appolonius circle, as desired.  $\blacksquare$

