Now, we show that if we have an  $N\times N$  peaceful configuration with no free  $n\times n$  squares, then there is a similar configuration for  $(N-1)\times(N-1)$ . To do this, simply delete a row and column, and if need be, add a rook to make the number of rooks  $N-1$  (this can be done by pigeon-hole). Therefore, our proof is complete.  $\blacksquare$

Let  $f(n)$  denote the maximum value of  $k$  for some  $n$ . I claim that  $f(n)=\lfloor \sqrt{n-1} \rfloor$ .
First clearly note that  $f$  is nondecreasing. Hence to prove the claim, it suffices to show that, for any positive integer  $i$ ,  $f(i^2) \leq i-1$  and  $f(i^2+1)\geq i$ , i.e. when  $n=i^2$  there exists a peaceful configuration where every  $i \times i$  square contains a rook, and when  $n=i^2+1$  there exists an  $i \times i$  square with no rooks in every peaceful configuration. Also call a set of squares a " $x$ -bar" if its height is the side length of the chessboard, and its width is  $x$ .

I will first show that there exists an  $i \times i$  square with no rooks in every peaceful configuration with  $n=i^2+1$ . Clearly there must exist a rook  $R$  which is on the lowest column in the chessboard. Consider any  $i$ -bar which contains  $R$ . Clearly there exist  $i$  rooks, including  $R$ , in this  $i$ -bar. Also, the number of  $i \times i$  squares in this  $i$ -bar is  $n-i+1=i^2-i+2$ . Note that there are at most  $i \times i$  squares which contain a given rook, and only one  $i \times i$  square which contains  $R$ . Hence the number of  $i \times i$  squares which contain rooks is at most  $i(i-1)+1=i^2-i+1$ , which is less than  $i^2-i+2$ . Hence there exists an  $i \times i$  square in this  $i$ -bar which contains no rooks in it, so there exists an  $i \times i$  square with no rooks in every peaceful configuration of  $n$  rooks.

Now I will give a construction for a peaceful configuration for  $n=i^2$  which contains no  $i \times i$  squares that do not contain rooks. Here is a construction for  $n=16=4^2$ , which generates readily ( $R$  represents a rook):
