Clearly it can be verified that this contains no  $i \times i$  squares which do not contain rooks, as desired.  $\blacksquare$

Let  $f(n)$  denote the desired number. It is obvious that  $f$  is increasing. We will prove the following Claims:**Claim 1:**  $f(n^2)=n-1$  for all  $n$ .
*Proof:* We firstly show that each  $n^2 \times n^2$  peaceful board has a  $(n-1) \times (n-1)$  empty subboard. 
Remove the last line and the last column of the  $n^2 \times n^2$  board, so we have a  $(n^2-1) \times (n^2-1)$  board. Split this board in  $(n-1) \times (n-1)$  boards (there are  $(n+1)^2$  of these). If none of these were empty, we would have a total of at least  $(n+1)^2$  rooks, which is impossible since we must have exactly  $n^2$ .

Now we have to show a  $n^2 \times n^2$  peaceful board such that no  $n \times n$  subboard is empty. 
My construction is the same as post #45's, so let me just copy it here:

