[/asy] $\blacksquare$ **Claim 2:**  $f(n^2+1)=n$ . 
*Proof:* We firstly show that each  $(n^2+1) \times (n^2+1)$  peaceful board has a  $n \times n$  empty subboard. Suppose otherwise. Remove the last row and the last column, and consider the remaining  $n^2 \times n^2$  board. Split it in  $n \times n$  subboards (there are  $n^2$  of these). Each of these subboards has at least one rook, so we have  $n^2$  in total. Hence, the removed last row and last colum must have in total at most  $1$  rook, and since each row/column has at least  $1$  rook, this  $1$  rook must be at the lower-right square.

Now, remove the first line and first column and act similarly. We obtain that the upper left square has a rook. Removing the first row and the last column, however, gives that the removed row and column together have  $2$  rooks, and the remaining  $n^2 \times n^2$  board has at least  $n^2$  rooks, so this sums to at least  $n^2+2$  rooks, a contradiction. 

Now we have to show a  $(n^2+1) \times (n^2+1)$  peaceful board such that no  $(n+1) \times (n+1)$  subboard is empty. To do so, employ our construction from the  $n^2 \times n^2$  case, just add a row and a column, and place the last rook at the lower right square.  $\blacksquare$ To the problem, note that  $f$  is increasing, so if  $n=x^2+y$  with  $2 \leq y \leq 2x$ , then   $$ x=f(x^2+1) \leq f(n) \leq f((x+1)^2)=x, $$   hence  $f(n)=x$ . Therefore, it is easy to see now that  $f(n)=\lfloor \sqrt{n-1} \rfloor$  for all  $n$ .

We claim the answer  $k=$  $\left[ \sqrt{n-1}\right]$ .

