For all  $n<a^2$ , we can take out the top leftmost  $n\times n$  square out of the  $a^2\times a^2$  square and add black squares as needed to fill in remaining rows/columns. 
For our lower bound, first, any  $n=a^2+1$  must have an empty  $a\times a$  square. For  $a=1$ , this is easy. For any larger  $a$ , consider the column  $C$  with a rook in the first row. Since  $a>1$ , there must exist  $a-1$  other columns either all to the left or all to the right of  $C$ . The rooks in these columns occupy  $a-1$  different rows (imagine that a rook on a square takes out the whole row), splitting  $(a^2+1)-a$  rows into  $a$  different sections. By Pigeonhole, there must exist a section with at least 
\[\left\lceil\frac{a^2+1-a}{a}\right\rceil=\left\lceil(a-1)+\frac{1}{a}\right\rceil,\]
or  $a$  rows. There are no rooks on any of these rows in these  $a$  columns, meaning that this creates an empty  $a\times a$  square. 
For any larger square, we can take out the top leftmost  $(a^2+1)\times(a^2+1)$  square, which will have at most one black square in each row and column. Therefore, the largest possible  $k$  is  $\left\lfloor\sqrt{n-1}\right\rfloor$ , completing our proof.

