<details><summary>Solution</summary>Let  $S_n = \sum_{i=1}^n a_i$ . We proceed by casework.**Case 1:  $P_1(n) \Longleftrightarrow \frac{S_n}{n} \le  a_n < a_{n+1}$**
we have 
\begin{align*}
S_n &\le na_{n+1}
S_{n+1} & \le (n+1)a_{n+1}
\frac{S_{n+1}}{n+1} &\le a_{n+1}
\end{align*}
So  $P_1(n) \Rightarrow P_1(n+1)$  **Case 2:  $P_2(n) \Longleftrightarrow a_n < \frac{S_n}{n} \le a_{n+1}$**
as we have  $S_n \le na_{n+1}$ , similarly as above,  $P_2(n) \Rightarrow P_1(n+1)$ **Case 3:  $P_3(n) \Longleftrightarrow  a_n < a_{n+1} \le \frac{S_n}{n}$**
The key claim is proving that  $\exists m \in \mathbb{N} \text{ such that } P_3(m) \Rightarrow P_2(m+1)$ .
Assume, FTSOC, that  $a_{n+1} \le \frac{S_n}{n}$  for infinite values of  $n$ . But this gives  $$ \frac{S_{n+1}}{n+1} \le \frac{S_n + \frac{S_n}{n}}{n+1} = \frac{S_n}{n} $$  So the sequence  $\frac{S_n}{n}, \frac{S_{n+1}}{n+1}, \dots$  is decreasing, but  $a_{n+1}, a_{n+2}, \dots $  increases without bound, contradiction.

Therefore, beginning with  $P_3(1)$ , we have   $$ P_3(1) \Rightarrow P_3(2) \Rightarrow \dots \Rightarrow P_3(m) \Rightarrow P_2(m+1) \Rightarrow P_1(m+2) \Rightarrow P_1(m+3) \Rightarrow\dots $$  as desired.</details>

