Starting with  $n=1$ , we see that

\begin{align*}
a_1 < a_0+a_1 \le a_2 \Rightarrow{} a_1 < a_2 < a_0+a_1
\end{align*}

For  $n=2$ , we have

\begin{align*}
a_2 < \frac{a_0+a_1+a_2}{2} \le a_3 \Rightarrow{} a_2 < a_3 < \lceil \frac{a_0+a_1+a_2}{2} \rceil \le \lceil \frac{a_0+a_1+a_0+a_1}{2} \rceil = a_0+a_1
\end{align*}

By induction, we see that  $a_{k-1} < a_k < a_0 + a_1$  must be true for all  $k \ge 1$  for there not to be an unique  $n$  that satisfies the problem conditions. However, this is impossible since all  $a_k$ s are positive integers in an increasing sequence. Hence, we are proved existence.  $\blacksquare{}$  $\textbf{Uniqueness}$ Let there exist the least  $n$  such that  $a_n < \frac{a_0+a_1+a_2+\cdots+a_n}{n} \leq a_{n+1}$  is true.

For positive integers  $k$ , we want the condition of   $$ a_{n+k} < \frac{(a_0+a_1+a_2+\cdots+a_n)+a_{n+1}+\cdots+a_{n+k}}{n+k} \leq a_{n+k+1} $$  Then,

\begin{align*}
a_{n+k} < \frac{(a_0+a_1+a_2+\cdots+a_n)+a_{n+1}+\cdots+a_{n+k}}{n+k} &\leq \frac{(n*a_{n+1})+a_{n+1}+\cdots+a_{n+k}}{n+k} 
&\leq \frac{(n+k)a_{n+k}}{a+k} 
&= a_{n+k}
\end{align*}

A contradiction.
Hence we have proved uniqueness in  $n$  and are done.  $\blacksquare{}$</details>

