Note that  $Q$  lies on segment  $BP$ , since  $\angle BAP = \angle ACB$ ,  $\angle CAQ = \angle CBA$ , and  $\angle BAP + \angle CAQ = \angle ACB + \angle CBA = 180^{\circ} - \angle BAC > \angle BAC$ . 

Note that   $$ \angle AQP = \angle AQC = 180^{\circ} - \angle QAC - \angle QCA = 180^{\circ} - \angle CBA - \angle BCA = \angle BAC, $$   and similarly,   $$ \angle APQ = \angle APB = 180^{\circ} - \angle PAB - \angle PBA = 180^{\circ} -  \angle BCA - \angle CBA = \angle BAC. $$   

Also, note that  $\triangle PBA \sim \triangle QAC \sim \triangle ABC$  by AA similarity, so   $$ \frac{PA}{PB} = \frac{QC}{QA} = \frac{AC}{AB}. $$  Now, by the Ratio Lemma on  $\triangle APB$ , we have 
\begin{align*}
1 &= \frac{XA}{XB}  
  &= \frac{PA}{PB}\times \frac{\sin \angle XPA}{\sin \angle XPQ} 
  &= \frac{AC}{AB}\times \frac{\sin \angle ZPA}{\sin \angle ZPQ},
\end{align*}
so   $$ \frac{\sin \angle ZPA}{\sin \angle ZPQ} = \frac{AB}{AC}. $$  By the Ratio Lemma on  $\triangle AQC$ , we have
\begin{align*}
1 &= \frac{YA}{YC} 
  &= \frac{QA}{QC}\times \frac{\sin \angle YQA}{\sin \angle YQC} 
  &= \frac{AB}{AC}\times \frac{\sin \angle ZQA}{\sin \angle ZQP},
\end{align*}
so   $$ \frac{\sin \angle ZQP}{\sin \angle ZQA} = \frac{AB}{AC}. $$  However, note that   $$ \angle ZPA + \angle ZPQ = \angle APQ = \angle AQP = \angle ZQA + \angle ZQP = \angle BAC < 90^{\circ}, $$   since  $\triangle ABC$  is acute. Thus, if  $\alpha = \angle BAC$  (in degrees), and  $f(x) = \frac{\sin x^{\circ}}{\sin (\alpha - x)^{\circ}},$  we have that  $f$  is increasing on  $(0, \alpha)$ , since  $\alpha < 90^{\circ}$ , since if  $\alpha > y > x$  (all in degrees. I won't put degree marks from now on.), we have  $\sin y > \sin x$ , and  $\sin (\alpha - y) < \sin (\alpha - x)$ , since  $\sin$  is increasing on  $(0, 90)$  (degrees), so  $f(x) < f(y)$ . However, we have that  $f(\angle ZPA) = f(\angle ZQP)$ , so we must have  $\angle ZPA = \angle ZQP$ . Then, we have 
\begin{align*}
\angle XZY &= \angle PZQ 
           &= 180^{\circ} - \angle ZQP - \angle ZPQ 
           &= 180^{\circ} - \angle ZQP - (\angle APQ - \angle ZPQ) 
           &= 180^{\circ} + (\angle ZPQ - \angle ZQP) - \angle APQ 
           &= 180^{\circ} - \angle APQ 
           &= 180^{\circ} - \angle BAC 
           &= 180^{\circ} - \angle XAY,
\end{align*}
so quadrilateral  $AXZY$  is cyclic, as desired.  $\blacksquare$</details>

