Let  $x_p$  be the **non-negative** remainder when  $x$  is divided by  $p.$  The result holds for  $p=2,3.$  So assume  $p \ge 5.$ **<span style="color:#00f">Lemma</span>:** For any prime  $p,$    $$ \sum_{i=1}^{p-1} i^3_p=\frac{p(p-1)}{2} $$  *Proof:* Simply note that 
\begin{align*}
2\sum_{i=1}^{p-1} i^3_p &=\sum_{i=1}^{p-1} i^3_p+(p-i)^3_p 
&= \sum_{i=1}^{p-1} i^3_p+\left( p-i^3_p \right) 
&=p(p-1) & \square
\end{align*}
Thus, 
\begin{align*}
\sum_{k=1}^{p-1} \left \lfloor \frac{k^3}{p} \right \rfloor &= \sum_{k=1}^{p-1} \frac{k^3-k^3_p}{p} 
&= \frac{1}{p} \left( \left( \frac{p(p-1)}{2} \right)^2-\frac{p(p-1)}{2} \right) 
&= \frac{(p-1)}{4} \cdot (p^2-p-2) 
&= \frac{(p+1)(p-1)(p-2)}{4} & \blacksquare
\end{align*}**P.S.** If I am not wrong, the fact that  $p$  is a prime is just a red herring right? This result should hold for all odd  $p.$

