Let  $Z$  be the midpoint of  $DE$ . Let  $BE \cap DF = \{Y\}$  and  $AD \cap EF = \{X\}$ .

In  $\triangle DBF$ , angle bisector theorem gives  $\dfrac{BD}{BF} = \dfrac{DY}{YF}$ .

In  $\triangle AEF$ , angle bisector theorem gives  $\dfrac{AF}{AE} = \dfrac{XF}{XE}$ .

In  $\triangle DEF$ , Ceva Theorem for points  $X$ ,  $Y$ ,  $Z$  gives \[\dfrac{DY}{YF} \cdot \dfrac{XF}{XE} \cdot \dfrac {EZ}{ZD} \Rightarrow \dfrac{BD}{BF} \cdot \dfrac{AF}{AE} \Rightarrow \dfrac{AF}{BF} = \dfrac{AE}{BD} \qquad (1)\]
In  $\triangle ABC$ ,  $a=12$ ,  $b=5$ ,  $c=13$ . Angle bisector theorem gives  $BD = \dfrac{ac}{b+c} = \dfrac {26}{3}$  and  $AE = \dfrac{bc}{a+c} = \dfrac {13}{5}$ . If we write these values in  $(1)$ ,  $\dfrac{AF}{BF} = \dfrac{3}{10}$ . Since  $AB=13$ ,  $AF=3$ .  $\blacksquare$ **PS:**
We did not use any right triangle properties. In any triangle,  $\boxed{\dfrac{AF}{BF} = \dfrac{AE}{BD} = \dfrac{b(b+c)}{a(a+c)}}$ .
We have  $c^2 = a^2+b^2$  in our problem. Since in every right triangle we have  $[ABC]=(u-a)(u-b) = \dfrac{ab}2$ , where  $u$  is semiperimeter, \[\begin{array}{rcl}
\dfrac{b(b+c)}{a(a+c)} &=& \dfrac{2b(b+c-a)+2ab}{2a(a+c-b)+2ab} 
&=& \dfrac{2b(b+c-a)+(b+c-a)(a+c-b)}{2a(a+c-b)+(b+c-a)(a+c-b)}  
&=& \dfrac{(b+c-a)(a+b+c)}{(a+c-b)(a+bc)}  
&=& \dfrac{u-a}{u-b}
\end{array}\] Since  $AC = b = (u-a)+(u-b)$ ,  $F$  is the point where incircle touches  $AC$ . Hence,  $IF \perp AB$ .

In fact, the solution of the equation  $\dfrac{b(b+c)}{a(a+c)} = \dfrac{u-a}{u-b}$  is   $a=b$  or  $c^2 = a^2+b^2$  (by simply multiplying and factorization). So in  $\triangle ABC$ , if  $AC=BC$  or  $AC^2 + BC^2 = AB^2$ , it is  $IF \perp AB$ .

