Suppose that after the first  $n$  coin flips, the number we have spelled out so far is  $\frac{k}{2^n}$ , where  $k$  is an integer with  $0\le k < 2^n$ . Then the final number we get, if this process continues for an arbitrarily long time, is going to be at least  $\frac{k}{2^n}$ , but no more than  $\frac{k+1}{2^n}$  (this is the limit you'd get if every single coin flip after that gave you a  $1$ .) And since we multiply by  $N$  and take the ceiling, the result is unambiguous if and only if  $$ \left\lceil\frac{Nk}{2^n}\right\rceil = \left\lceil\frac{N(k+1)}{2^n}\right\rceil $$  Now, applying a couple of ceiling/floor identities, we obtain  $$ \left\lceil\frac{Nk}{2^n}\right\rceil = \left\lceil\frac{N(k+1)}{2^n}+\frac{-N}{2^n}\right\rceil \le \left\lceil\frac{N(k+1)}{2^n}\right\rceil + \left\lceil\frac{-N}{2^n}\right\rceil = \left\lceil\frac{N(k+1)}{2^n}\right\rceil - \left\lfloor\frac{N}{2^n}\right\rfloor $$    $$ \left\lceil\frac{N(k+1)}{2^n}\right\rceil \ge \left\lceil\frac{Nk}{2^n}\right\rceil + \left\lfloor\frac{N}{2^n}\right\rfloor $$  This tells us right away that if  $N \ge 2^n$ , this is impossible. We have to flip the coin at least  $\log_2N$  times for there to be any chance of an unambiguous result. But then, using the same identity in the other direction:  $$ \left\lceil\frac{N(k+1)}{2^n}\right\rceil \le \left\lceil\frac{Nk}{2^n}\right\rceil + \left\lceil\frac{N}{2^n}\right\rceil \le \left\lceil\frac{Nk}{2^n}\right\rceil + 1 $$  in the case where  $n>\log_2N$ , and consequently the result is unambiguous if and only if  $$ \left\lceil\frac{N(k+1)}{2^n}\right\rceil - \left\lceil\frac{Nk}{2^n}\right\rceil = 0 $$    $$ 1 - \left\lceil\frac{N(k+1)}{2^n}\right\rceil + \left\lceil\frac{Nk}{2^n}\right\rceil = 1 $$  And this expression is guaranteed to be either  $0$  or  $1$  because of the above inequalities.

Now, this is where things get interesting. Define  $p_n$  to be the probability that it takes us at most  $n$  coin flips to get an unambiguous result. Thus  $p_n=0$  if  $n\le\log_2N$ , and otherwise:  $$ p_n = \frac1{2^n}\sum_{k=0}^{2^n-1} f(k, n) $$  where  $f(k, n)$  is  $1$  if the result is unambiguous for  $\frac{k}{2^n}$  and  $0$  otherwise. But we have just found an expression for this!  $$ f(k, n) = 1 - \left\lceil\frac{N(k+1)}{2^n}\right\rceil + \left\lceil\frac{Nk}{2^n}\right\rceil $$  And thus  $$ p_n = \frac1{2^n}\sum_{k=0}^{2^n-1}\left(1 - \left\lceil\frac{N(k+1)}{2^n}\right\rceil + \left\lceil\frac{Nk}{2^n}\right\rceil\right) $$    $$ p_n = 1 - \frac1{2^n}\sum_{k=0}^{2^n-1}\left(\left\lceil\frac{N(k+1)}{2^n}\right\rceil - \left\lceil\frac{Nk}{2^n}\right\rceil\right) $$  And this sum telescopes beautifully to  $$ p_n = 1 - \frac{N}{2^n} $$  Now, we derive the answer to the question from this formula. Since  $p_n$  is the probability that it takes at most  $n$  flips to get an unambiguous result, the probability that it takes exactly  $n$  flips is  $p_n-p_{n-1}$ . Thus the expected number of flips is
\begin{align*}
\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} n\left(p_n-p_{n-1}\right) &= \lim_{S\rightarrow\infty}\sum_{n=1}^S n\left(p_n-p_{n-1}\right) 
&= \lim_{S\rightarrow\infty}\left(Sp_S-p_{S-1}-p_{S-2}-\cdots-p_0\right) 
&= \lim_{S\rightarrow\infty}\left[S\left(1-\frac{N}{2^S}\right)-\sum_{n=\lceil\log_2N\rceil}^{S-1}\left(1-\frac{N}{2^n}\right)\right] 
&= \lim_{S\rightarrow\infty}\left[S-\frac{SN}{2^S}-S+\lceil\log_2N\rceil+\sum_{n=\lceil\log_2N\rceil}^{S-1}\frac{N}{2^n}\right] 
&= \lceil\log_2N\rceil + N\cdot\lim_{S\rightarrow\infty}\left[\left(\sum_{n=\lceil\log_2N\rceil}^{S-1}\frac1{2^n}\right)-\frac{S}{2^S}\right] 
&= \lceil\log_2N\rceil + N\sum_{n=\lceil\log_2N\rceil}^{\infty}\frac1{2^n} 
&= \lceil\log_2N\rceil + \frac{2N}{2^{\lceil\log_2N\rceil}} 
\end{align*}
If  $N=2014$ , then  $\lceil\log_2N\rceil=11$  and the answer is  $11+\frac{2\cdot2014}{2^{11}} = 11+\frac{1007}{512} = \frac{6639}{512}$ , so  $100m+n=\boxed{664412}$ .

EDIT: Well darn, apparently the correct answer is actually  $332156$  according to the contest website. This would translate to a fractional value of  $\frac{3319}{256}=\frac{6638}{512}$  so I guess I was off by  $\frac1{512}$  somewhere. Who can figure out where I screwed up?

