The states are which seat number you are after each move. There are a total of 10 states. Let  $P_1, P_2, \ldots, P_{10}$  be the probabilities to exit from the front. Let  $P_0 = 1, P_{11} = 0.$  We are looking for  $P_8.$  We can write the following equations:
\begin{align*}
    P_k & = \frac{kP_{k - 1} + (11 - k)P_{k + 1}}{11} \text{ for } k = 1, 2, \ldots, 10
\end{align*}
Thus, we can write the following finite differences:   $$ (11 - k)(P_{k + 1} - P_k) = k(P_k - P_{k - 1}) $$   Since the sum of them all is  $P_{11} - P_0 = -1,$  we have that  $P_{k + 1} - P_k = \frac{\binom{10}n}{2^{10}}.$  Thus,  $P_8 = \frac{\binom{10}{10} + \binom{10}{9} + \binom{10}{8}}{2^{10}} = \boxed{\frac{7}{128}}.$

Let  $p_k$  be the probability that a person whose  $k$ th in line exits from the front, rather than the back. Then we can derive that
\begin{align*}
&p_i = \dfrac{11-i}{11}\left(\dfrac{1}{2}p_{i-1}+\dfrac{1}{2}p_i\right)+\dfrac{i}{11}\left(\dfrac{1}{2}p_i+\dfrac{1}{2}p_{i+1}\right) 
\implies& p_i = \dfrac{11-i}{11}p_{i-1}+\dfrac{i}{11}p_{i+1} 
\implies& p_i - p_{i-1} = \frac{i}{11-i}(p_{i+1}-p_i).
\end{align*}
If we substitute each of the following equations into the next:
\begin{align*}
p_i - p_{i-1} &= \frac{i}{11-i}(p_{i+1}-p_i) 
p_{i-1} - p_{i-2} &= \frac{i-1}{12-i}(p_i-p_{i-1}) 
&\vdots 
p_{2} - p_{1} &= \frac{2}{9}(p_3-p_2) 
p_{1} - p_{0} &= \frac{1}{10}(p_2-p_1)
\end{align*}
then we get that
\begin{align*}
&p_{1} - p_{0} = \frac{i!}{\dfrac{10!}{(10-i)}!}(p_{i+1}-p_i) 
\implies& \binom{10}{i}(p_{1} - p_{0}) = (p_{i+1}-p_i)
\end{align*}
which is the main claim.
We establish that  $p_0 = 1$  and that  $p_{11} = 0$ . Now, letting  $d_i = p_i-p_{i-1}$ , we can write that  $d_1+d_2+\dots+d_{11} = 1$ . Given the equation  $\binom{10}{i}d_1 = d_{i+1}$ , we can then derive that \[\left(\binom{10}{0}+\binom{10}{1}+\dots+\binom{10}{9}+\binom{10}{10}\right)d_1 = 1\] implying that  $d_1 = \frac{1}{1024}$ . Repeatedly plugging this into the main claim, we will find that
\begin{align*}
p_8 &= \dfrac{\binom{10}{0}+\binom{10}{1}+\binom{10}{2}}{2^{10}} 
&= \boxed{\dfrac{7}{128}}.
\end{align*}

