We have that  $\angle ASX=\angle ASM= \angle ACM=\angle C + \frac{1}{2}\angle A$ .
By homothethy  $\mathcal{H}_{A,k}$ , where  $Y \xrightarrow{\mathcal{H}} B$ , and  $X \xrightarrow{\mathcal{H}} C$ , we have that  $\angle ADX = 180 - \angle C - \frac{1}{2}\angle A$ .
Thus we have that  $ADXS$  is cyclic.

Thus out of here we have that  $\angle ASD = \angle AXD = \angle ACB= \angle ASB$ , which implies that  $S,D,B$  are colinear points.

Now here comes this killing angle chase:  $$ \angle YTS = \angle MTS = 180 - \angle MCS = 180 - (\angle MCB +\angle BCA +\angle ACS) = 180 - (\angle BAM +\angle YXA + \angle SBA) = 180- (\angle YXA + \angle ADS)  $$  but the quantity in the brackets is equal to  $\angle YXA + \angle AXS = \angle YXS$ Out of here we have that  $STYX$  is cyclic.

Now by the theory of the radical center, when applied on  $(ABC),(STX),(AXY)$  we know that the radical center will be  $P$ .
Since  $(ABC),(AXY)$  are two homothetic circles with the same point, there will be a tangent formed at  $A$ , and we know that  $P$  belongs to that tangent.

