Just following the hints from above;
Let  $S_p(n)=\{k | kp \in S, kp \le n\} \implies kp \mid a^{f(pk)}-1 \implies p \mid a^{f(k)}-1 \implies u=\text{ord}_{p}(a) \mid f(k)$  so if we let  $U=\{k:= u \mid f(k), k \le \frac{n}{p}\}$  it follows that in order to prove that  $S_p(n)$  has density  $0$  it is sufficient to show that  $U$  has density  $0.$ We prove this by induction on  $\text{deg} f.$ So in order to lower the degree of  $f$  we need something like  $f(x+y)-f(x).$ Hence, partition  $U$  in sets  $U_1,U_2,...,U_m$  such that  $U_i=\{k | k+i \in U\}$  and let  $m>n.$ So now  $|U \cap [\frac{n}{p}]| \le \sum_{i=0}^m |U_i \cap [\frac{n}{p}]|$  hence dense of  $U$  really is zero, what follows from hypothesis.

Now consider some primes  $p_1,...,p_m.$  Clearly,   $$ |S(n) \cap [n]| \le \sum_{i=1}^m |S_{p_i}(n) \cap [\frac{n}{p}]|+R $$  where  $R=|S' \cap [n]|, S'$  containing only numbers not divisible by  $p_1,...,p_m.$ By above it follows that we can choose  $p_i$  such that  $|S_{p_i}| \le \epsilon \frac{n}{p_i}$  for arbitrary small  $\epsilon > 0.$ It follows that  $ \sum_{i=1}^m |S_{p_i}(n) \cap [\frac{n}{p}]| \le \epsilon \cdot \sum_{i=1}^m \frac{N}{p_i}=\epsilon \cdot u \cdot N$ Look at  $R$  now. Number of numbers  $\le N$  not divisible by  $p_i'$ s is  $N\cdot \prod_{i=1}^m(1-\frac{1}{p_i}) < e^{-c}N$  and we are to find  $c>0$ We have  $\sum_{i=1}^m \ln (1-\frac{1}{p_i}) < -c$  and also  $\sum_{i=1}^m \ln (1-\frac{1}{p_i}) < -\sum_{i=1}^m\frac{1}{p_i}=-u.$ So we need  $u\ge c$  and also  $\epsilon \cdot u$  to be very small. Fortunately,  $p_1,...,p_m$  tend to infinity and hence we can manipulate with sum of their reciprocals however we want. To sum up, we have   $$ |S(n) \cap [n]| < N\epsilon (e^{-c}+u) $$  So let  $c=u$  and let  $u=\frac{1}{\sqrt{\epsilon}}$  and so the conclusion follows.

We perform the shift  $(a,b) \to (a+1,b+1)$  to every pair that may ever appear on the board. Then, we start with  $(2,1)$  and our operation is, 
\[(m,n) \to \{(2m-n,m);(2m+n,m);(m+2n,n)\}\]
Under this shift, we claim that any pair  $(m,n)$  for which  $\gcd(m,n)\ne 1$  and  $m>m$  with  $m+n$  being even may be eventually written on the board. We first prove the necessity of these conditions.

<span style="color:#f00">**Claim :** If the pair  $(m,n)$  is eventually written on the board, then  $\gcd(m,n)=1$ ,   $m+n$  is odd and  $m>n>0$ .</span>

*Proof :*The last one is clear since if  $m>n$  then  $2m-n>m$  and clearly  $2m+n>m$  and  $2n+m>n$ . For the first note that, 
    \begin{align*}
        \gcd(2m-n,m)=\gcd(m-n,m)&=\gcd(m,n)
        \gcd(2m+n,m)&=\gcd(m,n)
        \gcd(m+2n,n)&=\gcd(m,n)
    \end{align*}
    Similarly for the second, 
    \begin{align*}
        2m-n + m =3m-n & \equiv m+n \pmod{2}
        2m+n+m =3m+n & \equiv m+n \pmod{2}
        m+2n + n = m+3n & \equiv m+n \pmod{2}
    \end{align*}

We now show that all such pairs are indeed eventually attainable which we shall do via induction. Let a pair  $(m,n)$  be called \textit{good} if it satisfies all of the above conditions. Assume that all good pairs  $(m,n)$  for which  $1 \le n \le k$  for some positive integer  $k$  can be written on the blackboard eventually. We show the result for  $k+1$ .

Note that it suffices to show the result for  $k+1 <  m < 3k +3$  since we have the operation  $(t,k+1) \to (t+2(k+1),k+1)$ . Note that for good pairs  $(m,k+1)$  with  $k+1<m < 2k+2$  we have
\[(k+1,2k+2 -m) \to (m,k+1)\]
where the latter can eventually be written on the board since it is clearly good and  $0< 2k+2 -m < k+1$  so the inductive hypothesis applies. Similarly for good pairs  $(m,k+1)$  with  $3k+3 > m > 2k+2$  (note that  $m\ne 2k+2,3k+3$  since  $\gcd(2k+2,k+1),\gcd(3k+3,k+1)>1$ ) we have
\[(k+1,m-2k-2) \to (m,k+1)\]
where the latter can eventually be written on the board since it is clear good and  $0<m-2k-2 < k+1$  so the inductive hypothesis applies.

Hence,  $k$  can be any odd positive integer strictly less than 2014 such that  $\gcd(2015,k+1)=1$  and we are done.

