We swap the sums and then apply Hermite's:
\begin{align*}
  \frac1\alpha\sum_{m = 1}^{1000}\sum_{n = m}^{1000}\left\lfloor \frac{m + \alpha}n\right\rfloor & = \frac1\alpha\sum_{n = 1}^{1000}\sum_{m = 1}^{n}\left\lfloor \frac{m + \alpha}n\right\rfloor 
  & = \frac1\alpha\sum_{n = 1}^{1000}\lfloor 1 + \alpha\rfloor = \frac{1000 + 1000\lfloor \alpha\rfloor}\alpha
\end{align*}
Thus, we solve when this is geq 1200. This simplifies to  $5 + 5\lfloor\alpha\rfloor \geq6\alpha\implies 5\geq\lfloor\alpha\rfloor+6\{\alpha\}.$  Define  $S(\alpha) = \lfloor\alpha\rfloor+6\{\alpha\}.$  We want to compute when  $S(\alpha)\leq 5.$  Observe that on the intervals  $(i + 1, i]$  where  $i\in\mathbb Z,$  we have that  $S$  is increasing(this is just excluding integers). Thus, by computing each value, we see that  $[\frac{25}6, 4]\cup[\frac{10}3, 3]\cup [\frac{5}2,2]\cup[\frac53,1]\cup[\frac56,0).$  This gives the answer  $\frac{1}{6} + \frac13 + \frac12 + \frac23 + \frac56 = \frac52,$  and so the answer is  $\frac1{200}.$

We swap the order of sums to get that \[S = \frac{1}{\alpha} \sum_{m=1}^{1000} \sum_{n=m}^{1000} \left\lfloor \frac{m+\alpha}{n} \right\rfloor = \frac{1}{\alpha} \sum_{n=1}^{1000} \sum_{m = 1}^{n} \left\lfloor \frac{m+\alpha}{n} \right\rfloor = \frac{1}{\alpha} \sum_{n=1}^{1000} \sum_{m = 1}^{n} \frac{m+\alpha}{n}-\left\{\frac{m+\alpha}{n}\right\}.\] Now, notice that the fractional part attains all values in the set  $\left\{\tfrac{\{\alpha\}}{n}, \tfrac{\{\alpha\}+1}{n}, \dots, \tfrac{\{\alpha\}+n-1}{n}\right\}$ . Hence, we may write that:
\begin{align*}
\frac{1}{\alpha} \sum_{n=1}^{1000} \sum_{m = 1}^{n} \frac{m+\alpha}{n}-\left\{\frac{m+\alpha}{n}\right\} &= \frac{1}{\alpha} \sum_{n=1}^{1000} \alpha+\dfrac{1+n}{2}-\sum_{m = 1}^{n} \left\{\frac{m+\alpha}{n}\right\} 
&= \frac{1}{\alpha} \sum_{n=1}^{1000} \alpha+\dfrac{1+n}{2}-\dfrac{n-1}{2}-\{\alpha\} 
&= \frac{1}{\alpha} \sum_{n=1}^{1000} \alpha+1-\{\alpha\} 
&= \frac{1000\alpha+1000-1000\{\alpha\}}{\alpha}
\end{align*}
which we require to be greater than or equal to  $1200$ . Hence, \[1000+\dfrac{1000-1000\{\alpha\}}{\alpha} \ge 1200 \implies 1000-1000\{\alpha\} \ge 200\alpha \implies 5-5\{\alpha\} \ge \alpha.\] Now, we let  $\{\alpha\} = \alpha-\alpha-\left\lfloor\alpha\right\rfloor$ . Hence, \[5-5\{\alpha\} \ge \alpha \implies 5-5\alpha+5\left\lfloor\alpha\right\rfloor \ge \alpha \implies 5+5\left\lfloor\alpha\right\rfloor \ge 6\alpha.\] We now case on  $\left\lfloor\alpha\right\rfloor$ 

- If  $\left\lfloor\alpha\right\rfloor = 5$ , then there is only a discrete solution.
- If  $\left\lfloor\alpha\right\rfloor = 4$ , then  $\tfrac{25}{6} \ge \alpha$ , so this has length  $\mathbf{\tfrac{1}{6}}$ .
- If  $\left\lfloor\alpha\right\rfloor = 3$ , then  $20 \ge 6\alpha$ , which has length  $\mathbf{\tfrac{1}{3}}$ .
- If  $\left\lfloor\alpha\right\rfloor = 2$ , then  $15 \ge 6\alpha$ , so the length is  $\mathbf{\tfrac{1}{2}}$ .
- If  $\left\lfloor\alpha\right\rfloor = 1$ , then  $10 \ge 6\alpha$ , so we have  $\mathbf{\tfrac{2}{3}}$ .
- Finally, if  $\left\lfloor\alpha\right\rfloor = 0$ , then  $5 \ge 6\alpha$ , so it has length  $\mathbf{\tfrac{5}{6}}$ .

Summing these up gives an answer of  $\dfrac{\tfrac{5}{2}}{500}= \boxed{\dfrac{1}{200}}$ .

