\begin{align*}(1-x+x^2)e^x&=(1-x+x^2)\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{x^n}{n!}&=\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{x^n}{n!}-\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{x^{n+1}}{n!}+\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{x^{n+2}}{n!}&=(1+x)+\sum_{n=2}^{\infty}\frac{x^n}{n!}-x-\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{x^{n+1}}{n!}+\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}\frac{x^{n+2}}{n!}\end{align*}
Now we will replace  $n$  by  $(n-1)$  in the second one and  $n$  by  $(n-2)$  in the third one. (Why?) On doing so we get: \begin{align*}&=1+\sum_{n=2}^{\infty}\frac{x^n}{n!}-\sum_{n=2}^{\infty}\frac{x^n}{(n-1)!}+\sum_{n=2}^{\infty}\frac{x^n}{(n-2)!}&=1+\sum_{n=2}^{\infty}\frac{(n-1)}{n(n-2)!}x^n\end{align*} $(\bigstar):= \text{gcd}(n,n-1)=1$ . We will be working around this, the general term:  $\boxed{\frac{(n-1)}{(n-2)!n}}$ , mainly with  $(n-1)$  and  $(n-2)!$  due to  $(\bigstar)$ .
[rule]**<span style="color:#f00">Case 1</span>:** If  $n-1$  is a prime  $p$   $\implies \text{gcd}(n-1, (n-2)!)=1 -  (*)$ . Hence, from  $(*)$  and  $(\bigstar)$  we conclude that, for this case no terms would cancel out and  $(n-1)=p$  will remain as it is, in the top. So, we're done.**<span style="color:#f00">Case 2</span>:** Let  $n-1=p^2$  for some prime  $p$  i.e.  $n$  is a perfect square power of a prime.   $$ n-1=p^2 \implies (n-2)=(p-1)(p+1) \implies (n-2)>(p+1) \implies (n-2) > p. $$   Hence  $(n-2)!$  contains  $p$ , and hence one  $p$  will cancel out and one  $p$  will be left behind in the numerator, so, done.**<span style="color:#f00">Case 3</span>:** If  $(n-1)$  is not a perfect square power of a prime; let's assume  $(n-1)=p\times q$ , where  $p>1, q>1$  and  $p\not = q$   $\implies (n-2)=pq-1>p$ , similarly  $(n-2)>q$ . So,  $(n-2)!$  contains both  $p$  and  $q$ , thus in this case the numerator will cancel out leaving just a  $1$  behind and we're through.**<span style="color:#f00">Case 4</span>:** If  $(n-1)=p\times q \times r$ , where  $p$  and  $q$  are distinct primes and  $r$  is some number. WLOG, let  $p>q$  so,  $q \geqslant 2, p \geqslant 3$  and  $r \geqslant 2$ . Note that  $(n-2)=pqr-1 > p,q,r$ . So, with the same reasoning we conclude that as  $p,q,r$  occurs in  $(n-2)!$  we would again get the numerator as  $1$  (after cancellation) and we're all set.  $\quad \blacksquare$ [rule]

