We will indicate with an arrow  $\to$  the direction the children are facing, and we will assign some special, larger arrows  $\Rightarrow$  that cover several consecutive squares in a straight line if all the children in those squares point in the same direction as the special arrow. Note that each of these special arrows contributes  $1$  to the count, and that each special arrow has length  $\le n - 1$  (it cannot touch two sides of the board, since one child would not see another). Hence, there are at least  $\frac{n^2}{n - 1} > n + 1$  special arrows  $\Rightarrow$  There are at least  $n + 2$  special arrows.  

<span style="color:#f00">**Example:**</span>
[asy] 
size(6cm);
int n = 5;
draw((0,0)--(n,0)--(n,n)--(0,n)--cycle, linewidth(1));
for (int i=1; i<n; ++i) {
  draw((0,i)--(n,i), gray);
  draw((i,0)--(i,n), gray);
}

// Function to draw an arrow in a cell (x,y)
void arrow(pair pos, string dir) {
  pair p = pos + (0.5,0.5);
  if (dir == "a") draw(p--p+(0.3,0), Arrow);
  if (dir == "b") draw(p--p+(-0.3,0), Arrow);
  if (dir == "c") draw(p--p+(0,-0.3), Arrow);
  if (dir == "d") draw(p--p+(0,0.3), Arrow);
}

// Top row
arrow((0,4),"c");
for (int i=1; i<=4; ++i) arrow((i,4),"b");

// Next rows (1 to 4)
for (int y=0; y<4; ++y) {
  for (int x=0; x<=3; ++x) arrow((x,y),"a");
  arrow((4,y),"d");
}
[/asy]

